{"name":"Landmark","hex":"#746854","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"landmark","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An object that marks the boundary of a piece of land (usually a stone, or a tree).","A recognizable natural or man-made feature used for navigation."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To officially designate a site or building as a landmark."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/landmark"}],"entries":[{"title":"Landmark","blocks":[{"text":"A landmark is a recognizable natural or artificial feature used for navigation, a feature that stands out from its near environment and is often visible from long distances."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Hodges_cape-good-hope.jpg/330px-Hodges_cape-good-hope.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landmark","wikidata":"Q4895393"},{"title":"Landmark (hotel and casino)","blocks":[{"text":"The Landmark was a hotel and casino located in Winchester, Nevada, United States, east of the Las Vegas Strip and across from the Las Vegas Convention Center. Frank Caroll, the project's original owner, purchased the property in 1961. Fremont Construction began work on the tower that September, while Caroll opened the adjacent Landmark Plaza shopping center and Landmark Apartments by the end of the year. The tower's completion was expected for early 1963, but because of a lack of financing, construction was stopped in 1962, with the resort approximately 80 percent complete. Up to 1969, the topped-off tower was the tallest building in Nevada until the completion of the International Hotel across the street."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/61/Landmark_Hotel_%26_Casino_Logo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Landmark_(hotel_and_casino)","wikidata":"Q7745429"}],"images":[{"srcset":"ht